#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" :question/math-academy-id "e-21611" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y' + y = -10$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{1}{y_{c}}\\frac{dy_{c}}{dx}dx &= \\int  - 1dx \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y_{c}}dy_{c} &= \\int  - 1dx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y_{c} \\mid &= -x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid y_{c} \\mid &= e^{-x + C} \\\\\n&= e^{-x}e^{C} \\\\\n&= Ke^{-x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}$.\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K$. Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary real constant. Taking $A=0$ also includes the zero solution, which was excluded during division by $y_c$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21612" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3$.\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\alpha=1,\\\\2\\alpha+\\beta=2,\\\\\\beta+\\gamma=-3.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" :question/math-academy-id "e-21613" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}2\\alpha=-2,\\\\\\alpha+2\\beta=5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-2x} - x + 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" :question/math-academy-id "e-21614" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y' + 4y = -8x + 6$\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x}$, solve the initial value problem." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x)$.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1$. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, and $\\beta$, we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}4\\alpha=-8,\\\\\\alpha+4\\beta=6.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAe^{-4(0)} - 2(0) + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA + 2 &= 1 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91d1688a-e8ff-523e-bc5e-197a45513b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91d1688a-e8ff-523e-bc5e-197a45513b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238166} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d7a430-dcba-5ba3-91bb-ccf7a6e07e80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fc5d25c-b291-5d77-bf05-80ed6712a38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332265" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 6y = 12$.\n\nFind the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For a constant forcing term, try a constant particular solution $y_p=B$. Then $y_p^{\\prime}=0$, and substitution gives $6B=12$. Hence $y_p(x)=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d09639d4-7ca7-5167-96d5-59ed6c4007c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d09639d4-7ca7-5167-96d5-59ed6c4007c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238170} :answer-field/id #uuid "185433c7-11c2-55a1-add6-78877678c714" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d8bb74-de23-5617-b920-d92d442130c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332543" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3y = 6$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Try the constant particular solution $y_p=B$. The equation gives $3B=6$, so $B=2$. Adding the complementary solution yields $y(x)=Ae^{-3x}+2$, with arbitrary constant $A$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "946a58f0-7fda-591a-962b-a3c3f4426cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\ln (7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228057} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5a9ea0-5527-5929-8d01-79dc82bf0f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228058} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b45141-c649-5a43-ba1c-3e06ed33f83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228059} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7c19f-65c4-5588-9ddf-a2033b23ee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228060} {:answer/id #uuid "661f729b-bf31-590c-ab1a-051603cfc5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-14x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7c19f-65c4-5588-9ddf-a2033b23ee38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228060} :answer-field/id #uuid "c491bc83-cb48-5ee1-af92-a1ed74a9acd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "562b8019-27f2-5021-a398-19bcd11af241" :question/math-academy-id "q-332701" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{\\prime } + 7y = 3x + 4$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The complementary solution solves the homogeneous equation $y_c^{\\prime}+7y_c=0$. Separating a nonzero solution gives $dy_c/y_c=-7\\,dx$, so $y_c=Ae^{-7x}$. Allowing $A=0$ also includes the zero solution."}]}
